INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - MORNING

A big kitchen, yellow-walled, with a scarred oak table and a coal range ticking. Window boxes of red geraniums. A rooster clock on the wall reads six-fifty. Pancake batter on every surface.

GRANDMA ROSELLA PIPER, seventies, small and iron-backed, stands at the range flipping a pancake the size of a hubcap. HAROLD PIPER, her son, forties, sits at the table in a clean work shirt and reads the paper. His wife JUNIPER, forties, ties a ribbon around their daughter MABEL's braid. MABEL, seven, kicks her feet. The youngest, TOBY, three, sits beneath the table with a wooden spoon.

Rosella flips the pancake. It lands on the floor. Toby lunges for it, Mabel shrieks, and the two children scramble for it together.

MABEL
Toby's eating off the floor!

JUNIPER
Toby, no!

HAROLD
(not looking up)
Floor pancake's the best pancake.

ROSELLA
Harold!

Rosella scoops Toby up with one arm and the pancake with the other. Juniper snatches the spoon. Harold folds his paper and sighs with great contentment.
